00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.290
Game of Thrones fans are used to shocking
moments, but some of the most controversial

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.950
in the series so far have been the final two
episodes of the series when Dany burned King's

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.440
Landing to the ground, massacring her enemies
and common folk alike, followed by her lover

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.780
Jon Snow stabbing and killing her in the throne
room.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.740
It was a brutal end for the Mother of Dragons
who'd always believed she was destined to

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.420
rule on the Iron Throne.
"So, my little princess, what is it you want?"

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.180
"My birthright.
The Seven Kingdoms of Westeros."

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.650
She finally touched the throne she'd desired
for so long and when she asked Jon to break

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:08.890
the wheel and build a new world together,
they passionately kissed but Jon stabbed his

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.961
queen in the chest.
Dany had actually foreseen her destiny at

00:01:12.961 --> 00:01:16.960
the end of the second season when she had
a revelation in the House of the Undying.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.640
In that vision, Dany walked through the destroyed
throne room in King's Landing, and after reaching

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:26.630
out to touch the Iron Throne she pulled her
hand away, a sign of what was to come.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.219
In the finale, she does touch the throne making
us think that perhaps reality will be different

00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:35.719
to the vision, but just as Dany feels like
she's finally achieved what she came for,

00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:40.979
Jon Snow ominously appears behind her.
Remember that in her vision snow was falling,

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:45.209
and it's Jon Snow who's here to prevent her
from ruling over the Seven Kingdoms.

00:01:45.209 --> 00:01:49.409
The snow in the vision also resembles the
flakes of ash that fell on King's Landing

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:54.729
after Dany burned the city down, a reminder
that when Dany said "I am not here to be queen

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:59.779
of the ashes", Jon Snow made sure that he
would literally stop her from doing that.

00:01:59.779 --> 00:02:03.819
Dany's death was also foretold in her vision
when she passed from the throne room, through

00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:08.789
a door into another world, signifying her
transition from this world to the afterlife.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:13.650
In the vision, she walks out beyond the Wall,
something which also came true in season 7,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:18.299
and then she enters Drogo's tent to reunite
with him and Rhaego, her stillborn child.

00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:19.799
"Maybe I am dead and I just don't know it
yet.

00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:25.180
Maybe I am with you in the Night Lands.
Dany's mention of "dark magic" in her vision

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.349
is interesting because it's Jon who takes
Dany's life, and it was Melisandre and her

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:33.239
magic which brought Jon Snow back to life
after he had died.

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:37.959
In the tent with Drogo, Dany also wonders
if she's in a dream, which is worth noting

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:42.030
because the Mother of Dragons has a history
of making her dreams into a reality:

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.740
"How many centuries has it been since dragons
roamed the skies?

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.890
I dreamt that if I carried those eggs into
a great fire, they would hatch.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:54.590
I'm no ordinary woman.
My dreams come true."

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:59.250
So, could Dany's tragic end in the finale
mean her vision of being reunited with her

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:02.269
Sun and Stars in the Night Lands has finally
come true?

00:03:02.269 --> 00:03:06.010
Well, you could interpret it that way, but
not if you listen to Jon Snow:

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.530
"So is there life after death?
Not that I've seen."

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:14.120
The way Dany dies at Jon's hand also relates
back to ancient prophecies about the Prince

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.959
That Was Promised that were first introduced
to the TV show by Melisandre.

00:03:17.959 --> 00:03:22.159
The prophecy, which is also known as Azor
Ahai in the books, was misinterpreted a few

00:03:22.159 --> 00:03:26.640
times by the Red Woman who first assumed the
Prince That Was Promised was Stannis.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.269
In the legend of Azor Ahai, the sword that
could defeat the Darkness could only be forged

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:34.829
when the hero drove it into the chest of his
beloved Nissa Nissa.

00:03:34.829 --> 00:03:39.709
After Stannis was defeated in the TV series,
Melisandre switched to believing Jon Snow

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:43.051
was the Prince That Was Promised.
But the show had also presented Daenerys as

00:03:43.051 --> 00:03:48.140
a likely candidate when a different Red Priestess,
Kinvara, spoke to Tyrion and Varys:

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:54.150
"Daenerys Stormborn is the one who was promised.
From the fire she was reborn to remake the

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.750
world."
And when Melisandre met with Dany, the Dragon

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:00.379
Queen asked her:
"And you believe this prophecy refers to me?

00:04:00.379 --> 00:04:04.560
Prophecies are dangerous things.
I believe you have a role to play.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.189
As does another...
the King in the North, Jon Snow."

00:04:08.189 --> 00:04:13.170
As Melisandre learned from her past miscalculations,
prophecies aren't necessarily what we assume

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.390
them to be and we shouldn't take them too
literally.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.889
As it turned out in the end, there were two
stages to the prophecy.

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:23.169
Many thought that the threat of an eternal
winter had been defeated when Arya killed

00:04:23.169 --> 00:04:26.340
the Night King in the final battle of the
Great War at Winterfell.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.240
But it was telling that, in the final episode,
snow began to fall on King's Landing after

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.889
Dany had taken the city, signalling that the
threat to humanity had not yet been fully

00:04:35.889 --> 00:04:38.419
defeated.
For the prophecy to finally be fulfilled,

00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:42.900
then, Jon Snow had to kill Daenerys.
The story we've been watching is "A Song of

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.900
Ice and Fire", and by the end both Ice and
Fire have been defeated.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:52.060
And it's only the combination of Ice and Fire
in Jon Snow, with his dual Stark and Targaryen

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.550
heritage, that finally brings the prophecy
to its conclusion.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:59.530
And there's proof that winter is finally on
its way to being over when Jon Snow and the

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:04.180
wildings open the gate at Castle Black and
march north beyond the wall, walking past

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:09.180
a little green shoot emerging from the snow.
In fact, that's also likely a nod to the title

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.990
of George R R Martin's seventh and apparent
final book, A Dream of Spring.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:18.150
As rushed and problematic as this season has
certainly been, the idea that Dany's death

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.789
was the way to bring peace to Westeros was
foreshadowed back in the first season in Dany's

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:27.349
encounter with Mirri Maz Duur.
When Drogo was dying from an infected wound,

00:05:27.349 --> 00:05:31.669
Daenerys turned for help to the Lhazareen
priestess, who then performed a blood magic

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:35.560
ritual on the dying Khal.
During the ceremony, the pregnant Daenerys

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:39.650
also went into labour and Mirri Maz Duur helped
deliver her baby.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:44.180
After Dany recovered from the birth, she discovered
that her son Rhaego was stillborn, and that

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:48.690
Drogo had been cursed to never recover.
Dany protested to the priestess about what

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:53.120
she'd done, but Mirri's response was that
she did it to stop Khal Drogo's son from ever

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.800
being born and the destruction that he would
have brought on her people and the world.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.840
"My child was innocent.
Innocent?

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:05.240
He would have been the Stallion Who Mounts
the World.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.699
Now he will burn no cities.
Now his khalasar will trampIe no nations into

00:06:08.699 --> 00:06:11.840
dust."
Jon Snow's murder of Daenerys mirrors what

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.270
Mirri Maz Duur did to Dany's son.
Each one killed an important figure to save

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:20.139
the rest of the world from the death and destruction
they thought they would bring.

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:23.930
When Dany objected to Mirri's actions, the
priestess scoffed at the notion:

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:27.220
"I saved you.
Saved me?

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:30.919
Three of those riders had aIready raped me
before you saved me, girl.

00:06:30.919 --> 00:06:34.900
In the streets I saw piles of heads. . .
the head of a young boy that l had cured of

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:40.900
fever just three moons past.
So. . .tell me again exactly what it was that

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.340
you saved?"
Even if we feel that Mirri was cruel to Daenerys

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.720
who herself was not directly responsible for
what the Dothraki did to Mirri's village,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.110
Dany's sense of righteousness about her destiny
has often been a blindspot.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:57.530
And when she burns down King's Landing, Dany
clearly doesn't see the irony of her words

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:01.010
last season that she wouldn't be "queen of
the ashes".

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:05.080
And after the slaughter her army and dragon
have brought on the city, she gives a chilling

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:12.300
speech to her troops, proclaiming them to
be liberators.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:21.530
So, Mirri Maz Duur did have a point when she
asked Dany what it was that she thought she

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:22.620
was saving.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.840
And if Dany's visions from the House of the
Undying, Azor Ahai prophecies and Mirri Maz

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:30.949
Duur curses weren't enough to warn us about
what the Mother of Dragons' eventual fate

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:34.020
would be, then there was also Jon Snow's previous
girlfriend.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:39.819
There was yet another big hint to Dany's eventual
death in the first episode of season 8 when,

00:07:39.819 --> 00:07:43.849
after arriving in Winterfell, she and Jon
went on a dragon ride together, in a moment

00:07:43.849 --> 00:07:46.970
that called back to Jon Snow's previous doomed
lover, Ygritte.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:51.800
"We could stay a thousand years... no one
would find us."

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:56.330
"Let's not go back.
I don't ever want to leave this cave, Jon

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:57.770
Snow."
It seems that being Jon's girlfriend means

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:00.080
your love for him will ultimately end up killing
you.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.500
Ygritte died when she hesitated to kill Jon
Snow during the wildling attack on Castle

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:07.539
Black.
"We should have stayed in that cave."

00:08:07.539 --> 00:08:11.110
Jon had abandoned Ygritte when he was forced
to choose between his duty to the Night's

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:15.090
Watch and loyalty to her.
And Tyrion's words to Jon in the finale are

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:19.070
a nod that again he'll sacrifice his love
for Dany in favour of what is best for the

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.530
realm.
"Love is the death of duty.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:25.520
Sometimes duty is the death of love."

